## Comprehensive Summary
The merits of intrinsic electrical conductivity, high specific surface area, tunable chemical composition and tailor-made properties enable two-dimensional conductive metal-organic frameworks (2D c-MOFs) as promising next-generation electrode materials in the field of energy storage and conversion. Herein, we have designed and synthesized a novel pyrazine-based 2D c-MOF (TPQG-Cu-MOF) bearing extended $\pi$ -conjugated structure and abundant redox active sites. Thanks to the excellent redox reversibility of pyrazine units and $\mathsf{C u O}_{2}$ units, as well as the insolubility of the rigid framework skeleton, TPQG-Cu-MOF as the cathode material of lithium-ion battery exhibits a reversible specific capacity $\mathrm{:}150.2\mathsf{m A h{\cdot}g}^{-1}$ at $20\mathsf{m A h}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1})$ , good cycling stability (capacity retention of $82.6\%$ after 500 cycles at $\mathsf{1}\mathsf{A}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1})$ and excellent rate performance. Comprehensive ex-situ spectroscopic studies revealed the reversible redox activity of pyrazine units and $\mathsf{C u O}_{2}$ units of TPQG-Cu-MOF during the ${\mathsf{L i}}^{+}$ insertion/extraction process. The deepening fundamental understanding of the structure-property relationship was proposed, which might pave the way for further development of efficient MOF-based energy storage devices.
## Background and Originality Content
With the increased reliance on portable uninterruptible power supplies and the demand of green and sustainable energy, cost-effective electric energy storage devices that store energy in the form of chemical potential through redox reactions such as lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) have drawn increasing attention.[1-8] Conventional commercial LIBs use graphite and transition metal oxide, respectively, as the anode and cathode materials, which however are reaching their electrochemical performance limits and may cause environmental pollution and resource consumption.[9] In fact, the performance of LIBs is more related to the cathode materials.[10] Recently, organic molecules containing redox active units such as disulfide, imide, aromatic azo and organic radical have emerged as an alternative to transition-metal oxide cathodes, due to their merits of flexible structure, rich sources, and environmental friendliness.[11-16] Despite these advantages, organic materials typically suffer from partial dissolution in the electrolytes as well as low intrinsic conductivity, resulting in limited lifetime and low rate performance.[11]
Coordination polymers (CPs) formed by coordination polyimderailzactainodnidbaettewteoenadmdreetasls itohnes abnodvebrisdsguinegs.[1o7r]gaAnsicanli geamndesrgianreg class of ${\mathsf{C P S}},$ two-dimensional conductive metal-organic frameworks (2D c-MOFs) composed of structurally designable organic ligands with special functional groups (‒OH, $-N H_{2},$ ‒SH, etc.) and elective transition metals (Cu, Ni, Co, etc.) are considered as the promising next-generation electrode materials in the field of energy storage and conversion due to their unique structural features.[18-22] First of all, the intrinsic porosity of 2D c-MOFs promotes electrolyte penetration and provides ion transport open channels, thus enhancing ion conductivity.[23-24] Second, the introduction of abundant redox sites and the proper adjustment of skeleton structure could optimize the energy storge performance.[25] Third, the strong orbital interaction between organic ligand and metal ion endows 2D c-MOFs with stable structure, which could control volume expansion and contraction during the charge/discharge process.[26] Last but not the least, the intrinsic high conductivity of 2D c-MOFs enables the fast charge/discharge process because of the rapid transport of electron to active sites.[27]
Pyrazine derivatives, because of their electron-deficiency that could interact with lithium ions, have shown great potential for energy storage and conversion.[28-31] For example, Dichtel and coworkers prepared a pyrazine-based DAPH-TFP COF, delivering both high-capacity and high-energy density when used as the cathode material of LIBs.[32] Herein, a novel pyrazine-based 2D c-MOF (TPQG-Cu-MOF) was prepared via the coordination between organic ligand (TPQ-8OH) bearing multi-pyrazine units and transition metal copper ions, which was further used as cathode material of LIBs. Notably, an in-situ cyclodehydrogenation of TPQ-8OH occurred during MOF synthesis. TPQG-Cu-MOF based cathode displayed a reversible specific capacity of $150.2\ \mathsf{m A h{\cdot g}}^{-1}$ at a current density of $20\ m\mathsf{A h{\cdot g}}^{-1}$ with a Coulombic efficiency of about $100\%$ . Moreover, excellent long cycle stability was achieved at a high current density of $\mathsf{1}\mathsf{A}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1},$ , with the capacity retention of $82.6\%$ after 500 cycles. Comprehensive ex-situ spectroscopic studies revealed the mechanism of ${\mathfrak{L}}^{+}$ insertion/extraction in pyrazine units and $\mathsf{C u O}_{2}$ units of TPQG-Cu-MOF. The TPQG-Cu-MOF is a promising cathode material for LIBs with excellent cycle stability and rate performance.
## Results and Discussion
## Preparation and characterizations of materials
The new pyrazine-based ligand TPQ-8OH was synthesized and characterized by nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectrometry and high resolution mass spectrometry (HR-MS) (Figures S1—S10). After systematic screening of solvothermal synthesized conditions, highly crystalline black powder was obtained by the coordination between the TPQ-8OH ligand and bis(1,1,1-trifluoropentane2,4-dionato-O,O‘)copper $((\mathsf{C}_{5}\mathsf{H}_{4}\mathsf{F}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{2})_{2}\mathsf{C}\mathsf{u})$ in a mixed solvent of DMF, MeOH and ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ $(V;V;V=2:3:1)$ at $85~^{\circ}C$ (Figure 1a). The high crystallinity of the as-synthesized MOF was confirmed by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) analysis. As shown in Figure 1b, the experimental PXRD pattern exhibited a sharp diffraction peak at $5.20^{\circ}$ assigned to (110) reflection together with four minor peaks at $7.90^{\circ}$ , $\pmb{10.55^{\circ}}$ , $13.80^{\circ}$ , and $27.65^{\circ}$ , which were attributed to the 210, 220, 410, 001 facets, respectively.
To further determine the crystal structure, the structure model with TPQ-8OH as the building block was constructed by Materials Studio software. Unfortunately, the simulated powder patterns of neither the AA stacking mode nor the AB stacking mode could match well with the experimental pattern (Figure S11). Instead, the simulated diffraction pattern of AA stacking mode with TPQG-8OH as the building block could agree well with the experimental result (Figure 1b). Therefore, it was inferred that an in-situ cyclodehydrogenation of TPQ-8OH occurred during the MOF synthesis.[33] To verify our hypothesis, the obtained MOF was digested by 2 mol/L HCl and meanwhile the possible digestion product TPQG-8OH was synthesized as a control sample for comparison. As expected, the NMR and UV spectra of the digestion product of the as-synthesized MOF were almost identical to those of TPQG-8OH, demonstrating the occurrence of cyclodehydrogenation of TPQ-8OH during MOF synthesis (Figure S12). Such in-situ cyclodehydrogenation of TPQ-8OH is probably attributed to the formation of free radical cations promoted by electron-rich phenolic hydroxyl groups.[34,35]
Then, Pawley refinement was performed to optimize the unit cell parameters $(a=b=33.84\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ , $\begin{array}{r}{c=3.21\mathring{\mathsf{A}}}\end{array}$ ; $\alpha=\theta=90^{\circ}$ , $\gamma=120^{\circ}.$ ). The final $R_{\mathsf{w p}}$ and $R_{\mathfrak{p}}$ values were $4.17\%$ and $3.22\%$ , respectively, which were within the acceptable range (Figure 1b). The porosity and surface area of the TPQG-Cu-MOF were estimated by nitrogen adsorption/desorption measurement at 77 K with a BrunauerEmmett-Teller (BET) surface area of $692.0\ \mathsf{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ and a pore volume of $0.82~\mathsf{c m}^{3}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ . The pore size distribution of TPQG-Cu-MOF calculated by nonlocal density functional theory (NLDFT) was 1.89 nm, which were consistent with the theoretical pore sizes of AA-stacking ( $1.60\mathsf{n m}$ and $1.92\mathsf{n m}$ , Figure 1c).
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) image and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) image showed that TPQG-Cu-MOF possessed a uniform particle morphology with a size varying from 50 to $100\mathsf{n m}$ (Figures 1d, S13). High-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) revealed the clear lattice fringe, which further confirmed the high crystallinity of TPQG- $\mathtt{.c u}$ -MOF (Figure 1e). The electrical conductivity of TPQG- $\mathtt{.c u}$ -MOF powder was measured using a two-probe method, delivering a conductivity of about $2.39\times{10}^{-4}{{\sf S}}{{·}}{{\sf m}}^{-1}$ at room temperature. Such a decent conductivity made it promising for LIBs. Additionally, the conductivity increased non-linearly with temperature from room temperature to $100^{\circ}\mathsf C.$ , revealing a typical semiconductor characteristic (Figure S14). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was used to probe the chemical environment of different elements of TPQG-Cu-MOF. The full XPS survey indicated that TPQG-Cu-MOF was composed of C, O, N and Cu. The high-resolution XPS spectra of $\mathsf{C u2p}$ exhibited a dominant peak belonging to ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ at $935.6\mathsf{e V},$ accompanied by a trace peak for ${\mathsf{C u}}^{+}$ at 933.6 eV (Figure S15). In addition, TPQG-Cu-MOF displayed a good chemical stability as the crystalline structure was retained after treatments in ten different organic solvents including 1,3-dioxolane (DOL) and ethylene glycol dimethyl ether (DME) for 4 d (Figure S16). The solid-state diffuse reflectance UV-vis-NIR spectra of TPQG-Cu-MOF showed a wide absorption range which was attributed to the large conjugated plane, and the bandgap was about 1.48 eV (Figure S17). The thermal stability of TPQG-Cu-MOF was estimated by thermogravimetric analysis (TGA). The mass loss of TPQG-Cu-MOF is about $12.8\%$ at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ which was probably attributed to the removal of free water from the material (Figure S18). Elemental analysis (EA) and inductively coupled plasma mass (ICP) quantified the content of C, H, N and Cu in TPQG-Cu-MOF, respectively, which finally determined that the structural formula is $\mathsf{C u}_{2}(\mathsf{T P Q G-}80){\cdot}12\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ (Table S1). These features enabled TPQGCu-MOF to tolerate the various electrolytes and different redox conditions, which may render it with excellent performance and cycling stability when used as the cathode material in LIBs.[36-37]
## Measurement of electrochemical performance
TPQG-Cu-MOF was further investigated as the cathode material for LIBs by mixing the MOF active material, conductive carbon black and polyvinylidenedifluoride (PVDF) with the mass ratio of 6 : $3:1$ . The coin cells were prepared with lithium metal as the counter electrode and $1\ m o l/L$ lithium bis-(trifluoromethanesulfonyl) imide (LiTFSI) in a mixture of DOL and DME $(V:V=1:1)$ as the electrolyte. Then the redox reaction of TPQG-Cu-MOF was assessed by cyclic voltammogram (CV) in the voltage ranging from 1.3 to $3.8\mathrm{V}$ at a scan rate of $0.2~\mathsf{m}\mathsf{V}\cdot\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ (Figure 2a). The open circuit potential of the as-prepared cell was $3.25~\mathsf{V}.$ The CV curves revealed TPQG-Cu-MOF exhibited redox peaks from 1.3 to $3.8~\mathsf{V},$ which was attributed to the presence of the redox active sites of pyrazine units and $\mathsf{C u O}_{2}$ units in the TPQG-Cu-MOF. Galvanostatic charge/discharge (GCD) profiles of TPQG-Cu-MOF cathode showed two sloping discharge plateaus, which were well consistent with CV curves (Figure 2b). The CV and GCD profiles of the next two cycles are completely coincident, indicating that TPQG-Cu-MOF electrode exhibited excellent reversibility. At a current density of $20\ m\mathsf{A h{\cdot g}}^{-1}$ , the charge/discharge capacities at second and third cycles were $154.6/149.0\quad\mathfrak{m A h cdot g}^{-1}$ and $153.7/149.8\ \mathsf{m A h{\cdot g}}^{-1},$ respectively, corresponding to a Coulombic efficiency of $96.38\%$ and $97.46\%$ (Figure 2b).
To further explore the rate performance of TPQG-Cu-MOF cathode, the charge/discharge capacities at various current densities were explored. Figures 2c and 2d showed that the specific discharge capacities of TPQG-Cu-MOF cathode at current densities of 0.02, 0.05, 0.1, 0.2, 0.4, 0.8, 1, and $2\ \mathsf{A}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ were 150.2, 139.7, 127.2, 118.7, 114.6, 111.9, 107.0, and $96.4\ m\mathbf{A}\mathsf{h}\cdot\mathbf{g}^{-1}.$ , respectively. Even at a higher current density of $2\mathsf{A}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1},$ the capacity retention was still $63.2\%$ . When the current density was changed back to $20\mathsf{m A h{\cdot g}}^{-1}$ , the discharge capacity could recover to $96.5\%$ of initial capacity and then maintained at $165.7\ \mathsf{m A h}\cdot\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , demonstrating the excellent tolerance of TPQG-Cu-MOF cathode towards the rapid charge/discharge processes.[38] The cycle stabilities of
TPQG- $\mathtt{C u}$ -MOF electrode were evaluated at the current densities of $20\mathrm{mAh}{\cdot}\tt{g}^{-1}$ for 50 cycles and $1\mathsf{A}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ for 500 cycles. A reversible special capacity of $135.7\:\:\mathsf{m A h}\cdot\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ could be maintained at 20 $\mathsf{m A h{\cdot g}}^{-1}$ after 50 cycles with a capacity retention of $91.1\%$ (Figure 2e). Moreover, TPQG- $\mathtt{\cdot C u}$ -MOF electrode exhibited an ultralong cycle stability at a high current density of $1\mathsf{A}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}.$ accompanied with a Coulombic efficiency of about $100\%$ and a low-capacity attenuation rate of $0.035\%$ per cycle (Figure 2f). The capacity contribution of conductive carbon black was $7.0\ m A h{\cdot}g^{-1}$ at 20 $\mathsf{m A h{\cdot g}}^{-1}$ , which was negligible compared to the total capacity (Figure S19). The lithium-ion diffusion behavior and the charge transfer resistance were further explored by electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurement. The Nyquist plots consisted of the semicircles in the high-frequency region assigned to charge transfer resistances and the sloping line in the low-frequency region related to Warburg diffusion resistances.[39] After 200 cycles, the TPQG-Cu-MOF electrode exhibited lower charge transfer resistances and ${\mathfrak{L}}^{+}$ ion diffusion resistances than that of the initial state, which could be ascribed to the good contact and the large interface formation due to effective wetting between the electrode and the electrolyte (Figure 2g).[40] These results indicated that TPQG-Cu-MOF electrode displayed excellent electrochemical performance with reversible specific capacity, good cycle stability and good rate performance.
## Exploration of the lithium-ion storage mechanism
To deeply explore the lithium-ion storage mechanism of TPQG-Cu-MOF, ex-situ FTIR and XPS measurements were performed at the different states during the charge/discharge process. $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{{E}}}\boldsymbol{x}$ -situ FTIR spectra exhibited reversible charge/discharge behavior during the redox process (Figure 3a). After discharge to $\mathsf{1.3V,}$ the peak at $1710~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ corresponding to the characteristic absorption peak of $C=N$ bond disappeared, and meanwhile the peak at $1262~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ assigned to $C-N$ bond appeared. While, after charge to $3.8~\mathsf{V},$ the signal attributed to $C=N$ bond recovered and that belonging to $C-N$ bond disappeared. Therefore, it was inferred that the functional $C=N$ linkages of TPQG-Cu-MOF were the active sites, which underwent redox reactions during the ${\mathfrak{L}}^{+}$ extraction/ insertion processes. Ex-situ XPS further confirmed the reversible de-lithiation and lithiation process of pyrazine cores and $\mathsf{C u O}_{2}$ units. In the Li 1s spectra, the lithium peak appeared when discharging to $\displaystyle{1.3\vee}$ and disappeared when charging to $3.8~\mathsf{V},$ which proved that TPQG-Cu-MOF cathode was extracted/inserted lithium ions during the charging/discharging process (Figure 3c).[41] In the N 1s spectra, when discharging to $\displaystyle{1.3\ V,}$ a decrease of the peak at 399.8 eV corresponding to $C=N$ bond and an increase of the peak at 400.7 eV belonging to $C-N$ bond were observed, accompanied by the appearance of a new peak at $398.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ ascribed to N—Li bond. After charging to $3.8~\mathsf{V},$ the de-lithiation led to the recovery of $C=N$ peak and disappearance of N—Li peak, further verifying the reversible coordination between the active site of nitrogen-atom and lithium-ion (Figure 3e).[31,42] In the Cu $2{\mathsf{p}}$ spectra, after discharging to $\mathsf{1}.3\ \mathsf{V},$ the area of the ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ peak around $935.8\ \mathsf{e V}$ decreased, meanwhile, the area of the ${\mathsf{C u}}^{+}$ peak around 933.5 eV increased compared with the pristine state. After charging to $3.8~\mathsf{V},$ the opposite evolution occurred (Figure 3f). In the O 1s spectra, the single peak shifted toward lower binding energy when discharging to $\mathsf{1}.3\mathsf{V},$ which indicated an increase in electron density of oxygen atoms. The signal peak returned to the initial position when charging to $3.8~\mathsf{V}$ (Figure 3d).[41,43] These results certified the $\mathsf{C u O}_{2}$ unit was redox-active unit for lithium storage.
Thus, a schematic diagram of lithium storing mechanism was proposed as shown in Figure 3b. During the discharge process, TPQG-Cu-MOF unit acquired six electrons and six lithium ions. Thereinto, the $\mathsf{C u O}_{2}$ unit was reduced from ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ to ${\mathsf{C u}}^{+}$ , and meanwhile the pyrazine cores acquired four electrons and coupled four lithium ions. A reverse process occurred during the charge process in which six lithium ions and six electrons were extracted from the fully lithiated TPQG-Cu-MOF. The deepening fundamental understanding of the structure-property relationship was proposed in Figure 3b, which might pave the way for further development of efficient MOF-based energy storage devices.
## Conclusions
In summary, we have designed and synthesized a new pyrazine-based 2D $c$ -MOF (TPQG-Cu-MOF). Bearing good electrical conductivity, large specific surface area and abundant redox active sites, TPQG-Cu-MOF was used as the cathode material of lithiumion battery which delivers a reversible specific capacity of 150.2 $\mathsf{m A h{\cdot g}}^{-1}$ at $20\ m\mathsf{A h{\cdot g}}^{-1}$ . Moreover, it showcased a good cycle stability retaining $135.7\ \mathsf{m A h{\cdot g}}^{-1}$ at $20\ m\mathsf{A h{\cdot g}}^{-1}$ after 50 cycles with capacity retention of $91.1\%$ , and $79.3\ \mathrm{mAh}\cdot\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $1\mathsf{A}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ after 500 cycles with capacity retention of $82.6\%$ . Ex-situ FT-IR and XPS revealed the redox reaction of the pyrazine units and the $\mathsf{C u O}_{2}$ units through lithium-coupled electron-transfer reaction. Our research implies the potential of pyrazine-based 2D $c$ -MOFs as cathode materials in LIBs, which could be further applied in other metal ion batteries.
## Experimental
## TPQG-Cu-MOF
$6.1~\mathsf{m g}$ $\left(0.01\mathrm{~mmol}\right)$ of TPQ-8OH and $7.4~\mathsf{m g}$ ( $0.02\ \mathsf{m m o l}\}$ of $(\mathsf{C}_{5}\mathsf{H}_{4}\mathsf{F}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{2})_{2}\mathsf{C}\mathsf{u}$ were dissolved in a solvent mixture consisting of $1.0~\mathsf{m L}$ of DMF, $1.5~\mathsf{m L}$ of ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ and $0.5~\mathsf{m L}$ of MeOH. After sonicating for $30\mathrm{\min}$ , the vial was placed in an oven at $85~^{\circ}C$ for $48\mathsf{h}$ . After the reaction was cooled to room temperature, the solid was filtered and washed with ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ $(10~\mathrm{mL}\times3)$ , DMF $(10~\mathrm{mL}\times3)$ and EtOH $(10~\mathrm{mL}\times3)$ , respectively. The solid was dried overnight in vacuum at room temperature, and TPQG-Cu-MOF was obtained as black solid in $92\%$ yield.
## The digestion of TPQG-Cu-MOF
$20~\mathsf{m g}$ of TPQG-Cu-MOF was soaked in $10~\mathsf{m L}$ of $2\ m o l/L$ hydrochloric acid solution, and sonicated to make solid disperse uniformly. After immersing for 2 d, the solid was filtered and washed with water several times. Finally, the black powders were dried in vacuum overnight at room temperature, which were used for the subsequent tests, such as NMR and UV-vis.
## The preparation of half-cell
TPQG-Cu-MOF, conductive carbon black, and poly(vinylidene) fluoride (PVDF) were mixed uniformly in a mass ratio of $6:3:1,$ and dispersed in anhydrous $N_{\l}$ -methyl pyrrolidone (NMP) to obtain a uniform slurry. Then the slurry was spread on carbon-coated aluminum foil, and dried at $85~^{\circ}C$ overnight. After that, the Al foil was cut into disk with a diameter of $9\:\mathsf{m m}$ . The mass loading of the active material was about $1.0{-}1.2\ m\cdot\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ . Lithium metal was used as the counter electrode, Celgard 2325 film was used as the separator, and the electrolyte was $1\mathrm{mol/L}$ LiTFSI in a mixed solvent of DOL and DME in the volume ratio of $1:1$ . The R2032-type coil cells were assembled in a glove box with the content of oxygen and water less than 0.1 ppm.
## Electrochemical measurement
LAND battery system was used to test the galvanostatic charge/discharge curves in the voltage range of $1.3{-}3.8\mathrm{~V~}$ at different current densities at $25^{\circ}\mathsf C.$ The cyclic voltammetry (CV) measurements were performed on an electrochemistry workstation CHI660E with various scan rates and voltage window of $1.3{-}3.8\vee(v s.\mathsf{L i}^{+}/\mathsf{L i})$ at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements were also performed on an electrochemistry workstation (CHI660E) in the frequency of $1000000-$ $0.01{\mathsf{H z}}$ at $0.5\mathsf{m V}$ amplitude at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ .
